Detailed implementation of Approach 1: Maximum Likelihood Estimation with Expectation-Maximization (EM) for recovering the connectivity matrix $W$ of a neuronal network using PyTorch on an A100 GPU. The code assumes the input spike data is stored in a 2D NumPy array loaded from a file, and $\tau$ is known. The implementation focuses on scalability and GPU utilization while handling the sparse nature of the data and enforcing constraints on $W$ (sparsity and row-wise sign consistency).
This code provides a framework for the EM algorithm, approximating the E-Step with a simplified optimization of hidden states $X_t$ and solving the M-Step with sparsity and sign constraints. Given the complexity of an exact Kalman smoother with a non-linear Poisson observation model, I use a gradient-based approach to approximate the E-Step, which is more tractable and GPU-friendly.

